body:
Empty string is used when the value is `undefined` for DynamicChangeDetection but not JitChangeDetection (Angular detections the best strategy on bootstrap). It might be a good idea to have default values 

``` typescript
class App {
  name: string = '';
  constructor(service: Service) {
    service.subscribe(data => this.name = data.name);
  }
}
```

Change Detection Strategy
1. PreGeneratedChangeDetection
2. JitChangeDetection
3. DynamicChangeDetection

@gdi2290 Is there a specific reason for the different behavior between DynamicChangeDetection and JitChangeDetection? If the detection mode is selected on startup, I'd assume it is because of some kind of feature/environment detection, but why would the end result be different for developers?

I agree with @gkalpak @pkozlowski-opensource that {{ bla }} should default to '' in the UI. I don't think having undefined or null visible in a UI is ever useful. Default values are an option but can be complicated assuming that the binding points to a class instance property.

Maybe having a hook to enable debug logging during development might be interesting (i.e., providing a way to let developers know that there might be something wrong).
